---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import A2uiSurfaceDemo from '../../components/A2uiSurfaceDemo';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<Docs title="A2UI Surface" description="Draws agent-made layouts from a safe catalog of Sprõut components.">
  <h1 class="docs-title">A2UI Surface</h1>
  <p class="docs-lead">
    Lets an agent answer with a layout instead of plain text: metrics, charts, tables, tabs and buttons. The agent
    writes{' '}
    <a href="https://a2ui.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      A2UI
    </a>{' '}
    messages that name components from a catalog. Your app checks them and draws them with real Sprõut components. The
    agent never sends code, styles or class names.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-8">
    <A2uiSurfaceDemo client:load />
  </div>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Two entry points</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>@tulipfarm/sprout/a2ui/catalog</code> has no React. Use it on the server to describe the catalog to a
        model, check its reply and turn the reply into text. It needs <code>zod</code> 4.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>@tulipfarm/sprout/a2ui</code> adds <code>A2uiSurface</code> and the adapters that draw each component. It
        also re-exports everything in the catalog entry.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">On the server</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Prompt, check and store a reply"
      code={`import {
  checkSurface,
  promptSnippet,
  sproutCatalog,
  surfaceText,
} from "@tulipfarm/sprout/a2ui/catalog";

const catalogs = [sproutCatalog, appCatalog];

// 1. Tell the model what it can use.
const system = [instructions, ...catalogs.map(promptSnippet)].join("\\n\\n");

// 2. Check what it sent.
const result = checkSurface(messages, { catalogs });
if (!result.ok) {
  // A2UI errors: { code: "VALIDATION_FAILED", surfaceId, path, message }.
  return retryWith(result.errors);
}

// 3. Keep a text version for copy, search and the model's history.
const text = surfaceText(result.surface, catalogs);`}
    />
    <ul>
      <li>
        Messages use A2UI 1.0: every message has <code>version: "1.0"</code>. Older versions are refused.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>checkSurface</code> refuses unknown components, props and functions, missing or repeated IDs, children
        that do not exist, cycles, parts not reachable from <code>root</code>, components in a parent they don’t allow,
        and links other than HTTP, HTTPS and mailto. Each error has an A2UI code such as
        <code>VALIDATION_FAILED</code>, <code>INVALID_RESERVED_KEY</code>, <code>INVALID_FUNCTION_CALL</code>,
        <code>EXPRESSION_ERROR</code>, <code>UNALLOWED_PARENT</code> or <code>UNALLOWED_CHILD</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Component names, function names, prop names and component IDs are Unicode identifiers: letters, digits and
        underscores, with no hyphens.
      </li>
      <li>
        Default limits: 300 components, 256 KB, 1,000 table rows and 2,000 chart points. Pass
        <code>limits</code> to change them and <code>allowUrl</code> to change which links are allowed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>catalogDefinition(catalog)</code> returns the catalog as an A2UI 1.0 catalog document, and
        <code>surfaceSchema</code> and <code>rendererMessageSchema</code> describe the messages in each direction.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">In the browser</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Draw a reply"
      code={`import { A2uiSurface, sproutAdapters, sproutCatalog } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/a2ui";

<A2uiSurface
  messages={messages} // append the agent's agentFunctionResponse here
  catalogs={[sproutCatalog, appCatalog]}
  adapters={{ [sproutCatalog.id]: sproutAdapters, [appCatalog.id]: appAdapters }}
  functions={{ [appCatalog.id]: { localTime } }}
  size="compact" // "full" in a side panel
  streaming={isStreaming}
  readOnly={alreadySent}
  onMessage={(message, metadata) => {
    if ("type" in message) openInCanvas(message.componentId); // "Show all"
    else sendToAgent(message, metadata); // action, callAgentFunction, rendererFunctionResponse, error
  }}
/>`}
    />
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>size="compact"</code> fits a chat message: charts are 240px tall and tables show 10 rows with a “Show all”
        button. That button calls <code>onMessage</code> with <code>{'{ type: "expand", componentId }'}</code>, or
        expands in place without a handler.
      </li>
      <li>
        While <code>streaming</code>, parts that have not arrived, and values still waiting for a function, show a
        placeholder. Afterwards, a part that is missing or fails to draw shows “This part couldn’t be displayed” and the
        rest of the reply still shows.
      </li>
      <li>Every chart has a “Show data” button that swaps it for a table of the same numbers.</li>
      <li>
        A <code>Button</code> sends an A2UI <code>action</code> with its event name, its <code>userMessage</code> and
        its resolved <code>context</code>. If the surface asks for it, the data model comes as the second argument.
        Without a handler, or with <code>readOnly</code>, buttons and fields are disabled.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Forms and checks</h2>
    <p>
      <code>TextField</code>, <code>CheckBox</code>, <code>ChoicePicker</code>, <code>Slider</code> and
      <code>DateTimeInput</code> bind their <code>value</code> to the data model with{' '}
      <code>{'{ "@path": "/name" }'}</code>. There are no password or secret fields. A component’s <code>@checks</code>{' '}
      list holds conditions and messages. A field shows its first failing message after it loses focus, and every
      message shows when someone tries to send. A button whose checks fail stays focusable but won’t send.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Functions</h2>
    <p>
      A value can be a function call:{' '}
      <code>{'{ "@call": "formatNumber", args: { value: { "@path": "/total" } } }'}</code>. Catalogs declare their
      functions and who may call them with <code>allowedCallers</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>rendererOnly</code> (the default) runs in the browser. If the app has no implementation, the surface sends{' '}
        <code>callAgentFunction</code> to the agent and waits for <code>agentFunctionResponse</code> in
        <code>messages</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>agentOnly</code> and <code>rendererOrAgent</code> functions may be called by the agent with
        <code>callRendererFunction</code>. The surface always answers with a <code>rendererFunctionResponse</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        A function with <code>requiresUserActivation</code>, such as <code>openUrl</code>, only runs from a click.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <code>sprout/basic@2</code> includes <code>formatString</code>, <code>formatNumber</code>,
      <code>formatCurrency</code>, <code>formatDate</code>, <code>pluralize</code>, <code>openUrl</code>, the logic
      functions <code>and</code>, <code>or</code> and <code>not</code>, and the checks <code>required</code>,
      <code>regex</code>, <code>length</code>, <code>numeric</code> and <code>email</code>.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Add your own components</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Add an app catalog"
      code={`import { z } from "zod";
import { defineCatalog, defineComponent, defineFunction } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/a2ui/catalog";
import type { A2uiAdapter } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/a2ui";

export const appCatalog = defineCatalog({
  id: "acme/chat@1",
  components: {
    Weather: defineComponent({
      description: "Today's weather for one city.",
      props: z.strictObject({ city: z.string().max(80) }),
      text: props => \`Weather in \${props.city}\`,
    }),
  },
  functions: {
    localTime: defineFunction({
      description: "The current time in a time zone.",
      args: z.strictObject({ zone: z.string() }),
      returnType: "string",
    }),
  },
});

const Weather: A2uiAdapter<{ city: string }> = ({ props }) => <WeatherCard city={props.city} />;
export const appAdapters = { Weather };`}
    />
    <p>
      Pass every catalog the agent may use. A surface names its default with <code>createSurface.catalogId</code>, and a
      component or function call from another catalog sets <code>@catalogId</code> (or <code>catalogId</code> on a
      call). There is no fallback: a name the chosen catalog doesn’t have is an error.
    </p>
    <p>
      Give each catalog a versioned ID. Within one ID, only add components, functions and optional props, so stored
      replies still draw. Change the ID for anything else.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Catalog</h2>
    <p>
      <code>sprout/basic@2</code> has these components:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Text: <code>Text</code> (Markdown), <code>Heading</code>, <code>Code</code>, <code>Badge</code>,{' '}
        <code>Callout</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Layout: <code>Column</code>, <code>Row</code>, <code>Section</code>, <code>Card</code>, <code>Divider</code>,{' '}
        <code>Tabs</code>, <code>Details</code>, <code>List</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Data: <code>Metric</code>, <code>Table</code>, <code>Chart</code> (line, area, bar, pie, donut),{' '}
        <code>Progress</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Input: <code>Button</code>, <code>TextField</code>, <code>CheckBox</code>, <code>ChoicePicker</code>,{' '}
        <code>Slider</code>, <code>DateTimeInput</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Values such as table rows can be literal, a function call, or <code>{'{ "@path" }'}</code> into the surface’s data
      model. A child list can be a template, <code>{'{ componentId, path }'}</code>, that repeats once per item of a
      list. Text that starts with <code>@</code> is written <code>@@</code>.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Accessibility</h2>
    <p>
      Headings start at level 2, so they sit under the page’s own heading. Charts name themselves with their title and
      can switch to a table. Badges, callouts and metric changes always say their meaning in words; tone only tints
      them. Placeholders are hidden from screen readers, and the surface is <code>aria-busy</code> while it streams. A
      component’s <code>@accessibility</code> sets its label, description, live region or hides it. Fields have visible
      labels, their check messages are announced, and keyboard order follows the reply’s order.
    </p>
  </section>
</Docs>
